  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mostly dry and comfortable weather is expected \nfor the rest of the weekend. A few showers possible tonight however.\n\nA drier and cooler airmass has arrived across the northern\nMiddle Atlantic region as high pressure to the northwest begins\ninfluencing our weather to a greater degree. Low pressure aloft\nacross the the Northern Atlantic continues to bring Northwest\nflow aloft over our area. A couple embedded short waves in this\nflow will pass across New England and our coastal waters later\ntoday and tonight. The activity will drift further south across\nDelmarva Sunday morning. We could see some scattered showers as\na result. Pops are generally in the 20% to 35% range, so no \nsignificant impacts are expected. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Minor tidal flooding is anticipated with Sunday\nafternoon's high tide, mainly for the southern Jersey Shore, \nDelaware Beaches, and Delaware Bay communities. A Coastal Flood \nAdvisory is now in effect from 3 PM to 9 PM Sunday.\n\nFlow will turn more onshore through Monday. This will result in\nwater piling up over consecutive tide cycles, leading to minor\ntidal flooding across portions of our area on Sunday afternoon.\nThe current forecast has minor tidal flooding from Atlantic City on\nsouth along the Atlantic Ocean coastline and for areas within \nDelaware Bay. With increasing confidence in minor tidal flooding\noccurring, a new Coastal Flood Advisory was issued for tomorrow\nafternoon for Atlantic, Cape May, and Cumberland County in NJ\nand Kent and Sussex County in Delaware. \n\nWithin the tidal Delaware River, northern NJ coast, and Chesapeake \nBay, tidal flooding will be limited to spotty minor or no flooding \nat all. Tide levels should decrease getting into next week as flow \nturns more offshore.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...A cold front will pass through on Thursday bringing \nthe potential for another round of showers and storms.\n\nBy Tuesday, high pressure will begin to shift offshore welcoming the \nreturn of southerly flow, resulting in a period of above-normal\ntemperatures and an increase in humidity levels through mid-week.\nHigh pressure looks like it will largely remain in control\nthrough Wednesday, before giving way to a cold front that will\napproach the area on Thursday. This front will be the next \nopportunity for any showers or thunderstorms around the area. \nHowever, moisture with the front could end up being limited so \nat this point POPs are only around 20-30% or less.",
